## GPU CEILING ~16-32 MiB: large GPU-initiated transfers fault the GUEST amdgpu
## driver, NOT the SPDK/rocm-xio data path:
##    BUG: unable to handle page fault ...
##    RIP: kgd2kfd_interrupt+0xc9 [amdgpu]
##     amdgpu_amdkfd_interrupt -> amdgpu_irq_dispatch -> amdgpu_ih_process
##     -> amdgpu_irq_handler
## i.e. an amdgpu KFD interrupt-handler fault under large P2P load (ROCm 7.2 /
## gfx1151 guest). The 16 MiB point already shows latency degrading (1.7 GB/s vs
## 5.6 GB/s at 4 MiB) -- the onset of the same stress. 16 MiB crash -> guest
## SOFT-rebooted and amdgpu re-initialized fine (SMU clean on soft-reboot). 32 MiB
## crash -> kernel oops cascade + `-no-reboot` -> QEMU exited (VM down; GPU now
## needs a host reboot to recover).

## GPU-initiated STORE: mode-0 host buffer is physically fragmented, so its PRP
## addresses don't coalesce -> SPDK 33-run cap (NVMF_REQ_MAX_BUFFERS) limits mode-0
## stores to ~33 pages. The contiguous path (mode-8 VRAM) is what scales. (4 KiB
## mode-0 store verified post-reboot: kvpool/6773616e346b size 4096.)

######################################################################
# PROTOTYPE: per-page VRAM P2PDMA addressing (candidate fix for the
# amdgpu KFD crash on GPU-initiated transfers > ~16 MiB)  (2026-06-12)
######################################################################
# Hypothesis: rocm-xio mode-8 treats the VRAM P2PDMA region as ONE contiguous
# run (single phys from REGISTER_BUFFER, which used only sg_dma_address(sgt->sgl)
# = first sg segment). A large hipMalloc VRAM buffer on this APU is physically
# NON-contiguous (carved from system RAM), so PRP entries past segment 0 point
# at the wrong pages -> NVMe DMAs to wrong memory -> GPU faults -> flood/garbage
# into the KFD interrupt ring -> stack smash in kgd2kfd_interrupt -> guest oops.
#
# Fix (3 layers, all COMPILE-CLEAN; kernel .ko built on host vs 6.19 as a syntax
# check only -- the guest needs its own 6.17 build):
#  1. uapi (src/include/rocm-xio-uapi.h): new ioctl ROCM_XIO_GET_BUFFER_PAGES (13)
#     + struct rocm_xio_get_buffer_pages_req {virt_addr, page_addrs, page_size,
#     max_pages, num_pages}.
#  2. kernel (kernel/rocm-xio/rocm-xio.c): handler finds the REGISTER_BUFFER'd
#     entry by virt_addr and walks its KEPT-ALIVE sg_table (same mapping => same
#     IOVAs), emitting one DMA addr per 4 KiB page across ALL segments.
#  3. userspace (src/common/xio-common.hip): the VRAM branch calls the ioctl after
#     exportRegVramBuf and populates bufferInfo->pagePhysAddrs/numPages, so mode-8
#     calculatePrps emits a CHAINED PRP describing the true fragmented layout
#     (SPDK then coalesces contiguous runs). Falls back to contiguous on failure.
#
# Caveat: if VRAM fragments into > 33 physical runs, SPDK's NVMF_REQ_MAX_BUFFERS
# (=33) coalescing cap returns a clean -ERANGE (no corruption) rather than crash.
# Whether the fix actually CURES the KFD crash is UNPROVEN until tested on the GPU.
#
# POST-REBOOT TEST (after stage-64mib-host.sh + launch-gpu-vm.sh):
#   # in guest -- rebuild module against 6.17 + reload, rebuild xio-tester:
#   cd /home/kyle/rocm-xio
#   git pull / re-scp src/include/rocm-xio-uapi.h kernel/rocm-xio/rocm-xio.c \
#       src/common/xio-common.hip src/endpoints/nvme-ep/*   # (host has the edits)
#   make -C /lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build M=$PWD/kernel/rocm-xio modules
#   sudo rmmod rocm_xio 2>/dev/null; sudo insmod kernel/rocm-xio/rocm-xio.ko
#   cmake --build build --target xio-tester
#   # then GPU mode-8 retrieve at 32 MiB and 64 MiB (objects already in kvpool/gpukv
#   # via `rados put`): expect "Resolved N per-page VRAM P2PDMA addrs" + no oops.
